Which next?

Yes — calibrate the gyro bias once while the robot is stationary, store that bias in EEPROM (with a magic/version tag), and subtract it from every gyro reading thereafter so you don't need to re-calibrate each boot.

How it works (short):
- While robot is perfectly still, sample gyro N times, compute bias = mean(gyro_samples). Formula:
  bias = (1/N) * Σ_{i=1..N} g_i
- Store bias (and optionally accel zero offset) in EEPROM with a magic value/version so you can detect corrupt/absent data.
- On boot: read magic; if present, load bias and use gyro_corrected = gyro_raw - bias; otherwise run calibration routine.

Minimal Arduino code (paste into your .ino — addresses chosen as example):
- constants:
  - MAGIC = 0xA5A5A5A5 (uint32_t at EEPROM addr 0)
  - ADDR_BIAS = 4 (float)
  - ADDR_ZERO = 8 (float, optional)
- calibration routine (stationary, N=500):
  - sum = 0; sumsq = 0;
  - for i in 0..N-1: r = read_raw_gyro(); sum += r; sumsq += r*r; delay(1);
  - mean_raw = sum / N; var = (sumsq/N) - mean_raw*mean_raw;
  - if var too large -> abort (robot moved); else bias_dps = mean_raw / GYRO_SCALE;
  - EEPROM.put(ADDR_BIAS, bias_dps); EEPROM.put(0, MAGIC);
- boot flow:
  - EEPROM.get(0, magic_read); if magic_read==MAGIC: EEPROM.get(ADDR_BIAS, gyro_bias_stored);
    else call calibrateGyro() (or wait for user to run CAL-ZERO).
- runtime: gyro_dps = (raw / GYRO_SCALE) - gyro_bias_stored; feed that to filter/PID.

Example snippet (concise — paste into your sketch):
#include <EEPROM.h>
const uint32_t MAGIC = 0xA5A5A5A5;
const int ADDR_MAGIC = 0;
const int ADDR_BIAS  = 4;
const float GYRO_SCALE = 131.0; // example: LSB per dps for MPU6050 at ±250dps
float gyro_bias_dps = 0.0;

void doGyroCalibration() {
  const int N = 500;
  long sum = 0;
  long sumsq = 0;
  for (int i=0;i<N;i++){
    int16_t raw = read16(GYRO_XOUT_H); // adapt if using different axis / function
    sum += raw;
    sumsq += (long)raw * raw;
    delay(1);
  }
  float mean_raw = (float)sum / N;
  float var = (float)sumsq / N - mean_raw*mean_raw;
  if (var > 200.0) { Serial.println("Cal abort: motion detected"); return; } // tune threshold
  gyro_bias_dps = mean_raw / GYRO_SCALE;
  EEPROM.put(ADDR_BIAS, gyro_bias_dps);
  EEPROM.put(ADDR_MAGIC, MAGIC);
  Serial.print("Cal done bias(dps)="); Serial.println(gyro_bias_dps, 4);
}

void loadGyroBiasOnBoot() {
  uint32_t m = 0;
  EEPROM.get(ADDR_MAGIC, m);
  if (m == MAGIC) {
    EEPROM.get(ADDR_BIAS, gyro_bias_dps);
    Serial.print("Loaded bias(dps)="); Serial.println(gyro_bias_dps, 4);
  } else {
    Serial.println("No bias saved; run doGyroCalibration()");
  }
}

// runtime use:
float raw_dps = (float)raw_gyro / GYRO_SCALE;
float corrected_dps = raw_dps - gyro_bias_dps;

Practical tips and tradeoffs:
- Sample size N: 200–2000; bigger N → lower random error but longer boot.
- Validate variance during sampling to ensure robot is still.
- Gyro bias drifts with temperature; if you see long-term drift, re-run calibration on user request or after significant temperature change.
- Store both gyro bias and accel/set zero if you want the boot pose to be the saved zero angle.
- Use a versioned magic value so future firmware changes can change layout safely.
- Provide a user command (CAL-ZERO or CAL-GYRO) to re-run calibration on demand; do not auto-run unless you can guarantee robot is stationary.

